617 \div 18 = 34 \text{ remainder } 5 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 617 \equiv 5 \pmod{18}
\] \[
943 \div 18 = 52 \text{ remainder } 7 \quad \Rightarrow \quad 943 \equiv 7 \pmod{18}
\] So the congruence \( 617 \equiv 943 \pmod{18} \) simplifies to \( 5 \equiv 7 \pmod{18} \), which is not true. This means that the original congruence \( 617n \equiv 943n \pmod{18} \) is true for all \( n \). The problem is actually asking for the smallest positive \( n \) that satisfies the original problem without canceling out \( n \). Since the congruence \( 617 \equiv 943 \pmod{18} \) is not satisfied, the smallest positive \( n \) that satisfies the original congruence is \( n = 1 \). </think> <answer> 1 </answer>
